     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": "That is a 94% reduction, consistent with the published figure that tool search \"typically reduces this by over 85 percent, loading only the 3–5 tools Claude needs for a given request\" on a ~55k-token five-server setup (GitHub, Slack, Sentry, Grafana, and Splunk). [1] The skills half pushes it further because bundled content is genuinely free until accessed: \"There's no context penalty for bundled content that isn't used.\" [2]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": "Skills get that shape from three-level progressive disclosure: metadata at startup, the SKILL.md body on trigger, bundled files and scripts only when read or run. The mechanism and its authoring rules belong to the prompt reuse objective, 2.5. What matters for a context budget is that level 1 is the only cost you always pay, so 30 skills is 3,000 tokens and 300 skills is 30,000 — and at that point the metadata is itself a context problem. [2]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "What the saving costs."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " One extra turn — the search — on any request whose tools are not already in the hot set. [5] Discovered tools persist in the conversation, so a second use in the same session needs no second search: \"The API expands "
       },
       {
        "t": "code",
        "v": "tool_reference"
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " blocks throughout the conversation history, so Claude can reuse discovered tools in later turns without re-searching.\" [1] So the amortized cost of the search turn falls as a session runs long, and is highest on short one-shot requests."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "What the saving buys beyond tokens."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " Accuracy. On MCP evaluations, tool search took Opus 4 from 49% to 74% and Opus 4.5 from 79.5% to 88.1%. [7] That is the part people forget: this is not only a cost optimization. Fewer competing definitions means better selection."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Where it goes wrong."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " Doing this arithmetic once, at design time, and never again. A tool library grows. The "
       },
       {
        "t": "code",
        "v": "tools/list"
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " on server six doubles after a vendor release, and nobody re-measures. Track definition tokens as a monitored number, not a one-time estimate."
       }
      ]
     }
